{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Router caches route loader data, but it does not manage mutation or\nsubmission state. Its role in mutation workflows is primarily invalidating\nloader data and reacting to potential URL side effects from external mutation\nevents. That said, we've compiled a list of mutation-related features you might\nfind useful and libraries that implement them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Look for and use mutation utilities that support:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handling and caching submission state"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Providing both local and global optimistic UI support"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in hooks to wire up invalidation (or automatically support it)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handling multiple in-flight mutations at once"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Organizing mutation state as a globally accessible resource"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Submission state history and garbage collection"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some suggested libraries:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/react/guides/mutations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://swr.vercel.app/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SWR"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://redux-toolkit.js.org/rtk-query/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RTK Query"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://formidable.com/open-source/urql/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"urql"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://relay.dev/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Relay"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://www.apollographql.com/docs/react/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apollo"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Or, even..."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://zustand-demo.pmnd.rs/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zustand"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://jotai.org/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Jotai"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://recoiljs.org/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Recoil"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://redux.js.org/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Redux"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Similar to data fetching, mutation state isn't a one-size-fits-all solution, so you'll need to pick a solution that fits your needs and your team's needs. We recommend trying out a few different solutions and seeing what works best for you."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"⚠️ Still here? Submission state is an interesting topic when it comes to persistence. Do you keep every mutation around forever? How do you know when to get rid of it? What if the user navigates away from the screen and then back? Let's dig in!"}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"invalidating-tanstack-router-after-a-mutation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Invalidating TanStack Router after a mutation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Router comes with short-term caching built in. Loader data can remain\ncached after a route match is unmounted, so a mutation can make both active and\ncached route data stale."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When mutations related to loader data are made, we can use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"router.invalidate"},{"type":"text","value":"\nto invalidate committed, cached, and in-flight loader generations. Matching\nactive preload lanes are retired, and selected current active matches reload\nthrough the normal loading protocol:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const router = useRouter()\n\nconst addTodo = async (todo: Todo) => {\n  try {\n    await api.addTodo()\n    router.invalidate()\n  } catch {\n    //\n  }\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, stale successful loader data revalidates in the background, so\nexisting data remains visible until the new data is ready. Cached inactive\nmatches remain marked stale and reload when they are reused."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want to await the invalidation until all loaders have finished, pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{sync: true}"},{"type":"text","value":" into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"router.invalidate"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const router = useRouter()\n\nconst addTodo = async (todo: Todo) => {\n  try {\n    await api.addTodo()\n    await router.invalidate({ sync: true })\n  } catch {\n    //\n  }\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"long-term-mutation-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Long-term mutation State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Regardless of the mutation library used, mutations often create state related to their submission. While most mutations are set-and-forget, some mutation states are more long-lived, either to support optimistic UI or to provide feedback to the user about the status of their submissions. Most state managers will correctly keep this submission state around and expose it to make it possible to show UI elements like loading spinners, success messages, error messages, etc."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let's consider the following interactions:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"User navigates to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/posts/123/edit"},{"type":"text","value":" screen to edit a post"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"User edits the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"123"},{"type":"text","value":" post and upon success, sees a success message below the editor that the post was updated"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"User navigates to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/posts"},{"type":"text","value":" screen"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"User navigates back to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/posts/123/edit"},{"type":"text","value":" screen again"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without notifying your mutation management library about the route change, it's possible that your submission state could still be around and your user would still see the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"Post updated successfully\""}]},{"type":"text","value":" message when they return to the previous screen. This is not ideal. Obviously, our intent wasn't to keep this mutation state around forever, right?!"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-mutation-keys","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using mutation keys"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hopefully and hypothetically, the easiest way is for your mutation library to support a keying mechanism that will allow your mutations's state to be reset when the key changes:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const routeApi = getRouteApi('/room/$roomId/chat')\n\nfunction ChatRoom() {\n  const { roomId } = routeApi.useParams()\n\n  const sendMessageMutation = useCoolMutation({\n    fn: sendMessage,\n    // Clear the mutation state when the roomId changes\n    // including any submission state\n    key: ['sendMessage', roomId],\n  })\n\n  // Fire off a bunch of messages\n  const test = () => {\n    sendMessageMutation.mutate({ roomId, message: 'Hello!' })\n    sendMessageMutation.mutate({ roomId, message: 'How are you?' })\n    sendMessageMutation.mutate({ roomId, message: 'Goodbye!' })\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      {sendMessageMutation.submissions.map((submission) => {\n        return (\n          <div>\n            <div>{submission.status}</div>\n            <div>{submission.message}</div>\n          </div>\n        )\n      })}\n    </>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-the-router-subscribe-method","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"router.subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" method"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./router-events.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Router Events guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a more complete walkthrough of the available events and when to use them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For libraries that don't have a keying mechanism, we'll likely need to manually reset the mutation state when the user navigates away from the screen. To solve this, we can use TanStack Router's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"invalidate"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" method to clear mutation states when the user is no longer in need of them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"router.subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" method is a function that subscribes a callback to various router events. The event in particular that we'll use here is the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onResolved"},{"type":"text","value":" event. It's important to understand that this event is fired when the location path is "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"changed (not just reloaded) and has finally resolved"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is a great place to reset your old mutation states. Here's an example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const router = createRouter()\nconst coolMutationCache = createCoolMutationCache()\n\nconst unsubscribeFn = router.subscribe('onResolved', () => {\n  // Reset mutation states when the route changes\n  coolMutationCache.clear()\n})","lang":"tsx"}],"headings":[{"id":"invalidating-tanstack-router-after-a-mutation","text":"Invalidating TanStack Router after a mutation","level":2},{"id":"long-term-mutation-state","text":"Long-term mutation State","level":2},{"id":"using-mutation-keys","text":"Using mutation keys","level":2},{"id":"using-the-router-subscribe-method","text":"Using the router.subscribe method","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Router caches route loader data, but it does not manage mutation or\nsubmission state. Its role in mutation workflows is primarily invalidating\nloader data and reacting to potential URL side effects from external mutation\nevents. That said, we've compiled a list of mutation-related features you might\nfind useful and libraries that implement them.\n\nLook for and use mutation utilities that support:\n\n- Handling and caching submission state\n- Providing both local and global optimistic UI support\n- Built-in hooks to wire up invalidation (or automatically support it)\n- Handling multiple in-flight mutations at once\n- Organizing mutation state as a globally accessible resource\n- Submission state history and garbage collection\n\nSome suggested libraries:\n\n- [TanStack Query](https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/react/guides/mutations)\n- [SWR](https://swr.vercel.app/)\n- [RTK Query](https://redux-toolkit.js.org/rtk-query/overview)\n- [urql](https://formidable.com/open-source/urql/)\n- [Relay](https://relay.dev/)\n- [Apollo](https://www.apollographql.com/docs/react/)\n\nOr, even...\n\n- [Zustand](https://zustand-demo.pmnd.rs/)\n- [Jotai](https://jotai.org/)\n- [Recoil](https://recoiljs.org/)\n- [Redux](https://redux.js.org/)\n\nSimilar to data fetching, mutation state isn't a one-size-fits-all solution, so you'll need to pick a solution that fits your needs and your team's needs. We recommend trying out a few different solutions and seeing what works best for you.\n\n> ⚠️ Still here? Submission state is an interesting topic when it comes to persistence. Do you keep every mutation around forever? How do you know when to get rid of it? What if the user navigates away from the screen and then back? Let's dig in!\n\n## Invalidating TanStack Router after a mutation\n\nTanStack Router comes with short-term caching built in. Loader data can remain\ncached after a route match is unmounted, so a mutation can make both active and\ncached route data stale.\n\nWhen mutations related to loader data are made, we can use `router.invalidate`\nto invalidate committed, cached, and in-flight loader generations. Matching\nactive preload lanes are retired, and selected current active matches reload\nthrough the normal loading protocol:\n\n```tsx\nconst router = useRouter()\n\nconst addTodo = async (todo: Todo) => {\n  try {\n    await api.addTodo()\n    router.invalidate()\n  } catch {\n    //\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBy default, stale successful loader data revalidates in the background, so\nexisting data remains visible until the new data is ready. Cached inactive\nmatches remain marked stale and reload when they are reused.\n\nIf you want to await the invalidation until all loaders have finished, pass `{sync: true}` into `router.invalidate`:\n\n```tsx\nconst router = useRouter()\n\nconst addTodo = async (todo: Todo) => {\n  try {\n    await api.addTodo()\n    await router.invalidate({ sync: true })\n  } catch {\n    //\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Long-term mutation State\n\nRegardless of the mutation library used, mutations often create state related to their submission. While most mutations are set-and-forget, some mutation states are more long-lived, either to support optimistic UI or to provide feedback to the user about the status of their submissions. Most state managers will correctly keep this submission state around and expose it to make it possible to show UI elements like loading spinners, success messages, error messages, etc.\n\nLet's consider the following interactions:\n\n- User navigates to the `/posts/123/edit` screen to edit a post\n- User edits the `123` post and upon success, sees a success message below the editor that the post was updated\n- User navigates to the `/posts` screen\n- User navigates back to the `/posts/123/edit` screen again\n\nWithout notifying your mutation management library about the route change, it's possible that your submission state could still be around and your user would still see the **\"Post updated successfully\"** message when they return to the previous screen. This is not ideal. Obviously, our intent wasn't to keep this mutation state around forever, right?!\n\n## Using mutation keys\n\nHopefully and hypothetically, the easiest way is for your mutation library to support a keying mechanism that will allow your mutations's state to be reset when the key changes:\n\n```tsx\nconst routeApi = getRouteApi('/room/$roomId/chat')\n\nfunction ChatRoom() {\n  const { roomId } = routeApi.useParams()\n\n  const sendMessageMutation = useCoolMutation({\n    fn: sendMessage,\n    // Clear the mutation state when the roomId changes\n    // including any submission state\n    key: ['sendMessage', roomId],\n  })\n\n  // Fire off a bunch of messages\n  const test = () => {\n    sendMessageMutation.mutate({ roomId, message: 'Hello!' })\n    sendMessageMutation.mutate({ roomId, message: 'How are you?' })\n    sendMessageMutation.mutate({ roomId, message: 'Goodbye!' })\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      {sendMessageMutation.submissions.map((submission) => {\n        return (\n          <div>\n            <div>{submission.status}</div>\n            <div>{submission.message}</div>\n          </div>\n        )\n      })}\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Using the `router.subscribe` method\n\nSee the [Router Events guide](./router-events.md) for a more complete walkthrough of the available events and when to use them.\n\nFor libraries that don't have a keying mechanism, we'll likely need to manually reset the mutation state when the user navigates away from the screen. To solve this, we can use TanStack Router's `invalidate` and `subscribe` method to clear mutation states when the user is no longer in need of them.\n\nThe `router.subscribe` method is a function that subscribes a callback to various router events. The event in particular that we'll use here is the `onResolved` event. It's important to understand that this event is fired when the location path is _changed (not just reloaded) and has finally resolved_.\n\nThis is a great place to reset your old mutation states. Here's an example:\n\n```tsx\nconst router = createRouter()\nconst coolMutationCache = createCoolMutationCache()\n\nconst unsubscribeFn = router.subscribe('onResolved', () => {\n  // Reset mutation states when the route changes\n  coolMutationCache.clear()\n})\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Data Mutations\n---\n\nTanStack Router caches route loader data, but it does not manage mutation or\nsubmission state. Its role in mutation workflows is primarily invalidating\nloader data and reacting to potential URL side effects from external mutation\nevents. That said, we've compiled a list of mutation-related features you might\nfind useful and libraries that implement them.\n\nLook for and use mutation utilities that support:\n\n- Handling and caching submission state\n- Providing both local and global optimistic UI support\n- Built-in hooks to wire up invalidation (or automatically support it)\n- Handling multiple in-flight mutations at once\n- Organizing mutation state as a globally accessible resource\n- Submission state history and garbage collection\n\nSome suggested libraries:\n\n- [TanStack Query](https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/react/guides/mutations)\n- [SWR](https://swr.vercel.app/)\n- [RTK Query](https://redux-toolkit.js.org/rtk-query/overview)\n- [urql](https://formidable.com/open-source/urql/)\n- [Relay](https://relay.dev/)\n- [Apollo](https://www.apollographql.com/docs/react/)\n\nOr, even...\n\n- [Zustand](https://zustand-demo.pmnd.rs/)\n- [Jotai](https://jotai.org/)\n- [Recoil](https://recoiljs.org/)\n- [Redux](https://redux.js.org/)\n\nSimilar to data fetching, mutation state isn't a one-size-fits-all solution, so you'll need to pick a solution that fits your needs and your team's needs. We recommend trying out a few different solutions and seeing what works best for you.\n\n> ⚠️ Still here? Submission state is an interesting topic when it comes to persistence. Do you keep every mutation around forever? How do you know when to get rid of it? What if the user navigates away from the screen and then back? Let's dig in!\n\n## Invalidating TanStack Router after a mutation\n\nTanStack Router comes with short-term caching built in. Loader data can remain\ncached after a route match is unmounted, so a mutation can make both active and\ncached route data stale.\n\nWhen mutations related to loader data are made, we can use `router.invalidate`\nto invalidate committed, cached, and in-flight loader generations. Matching\nactive preload lanes are retired, and selected current active matches reload\nthrough the normal loading protocol:\n\n```tsx\nconst router = useRouter()\n\nconst addTodo = async (todo: Todo) => {\n  try {\n    await api.addTodo()\n    router.invalidate()\n  } catch {\n    //\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBy default, stale successful loader data revalidates in the background, so\nexisting data remains visible until the new data is ready. Cached inactive\nmatches remain marked stale and reload when they are reused.\n\nIf you want to await the invalidation until all loaders have finished, pass `{sync: true}` into `router.invalidate`:\n\n```tsx\nconst router = useRouter()\n\nconst addTodo = async (todo: Todo) => {\n  try {\n    await api.addTodo()\n    await router.invalidate({ sync: true })\n  } catch {\n    //\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Long-term mutation State\n\nRegardless of the mutation library used, mutations often create state related to their submission. While most mutations are set-and-forget, some mutation states are more long-lived, either to support optimistic UI or to provide feedback to the user about the status of their submissions. Most state managers will correctly keep this submission state around and expose it to make it possible to show UI elements like loading spinners, success messages, error messages, etc.\n\nLet's consider the following interactions:\n\n- User navigates to the `/posts/123/edit` screen to edit a post\n- User edits the `123` post and upon success, sees a success message below the editor that the post was updated\n- User navigates to the `/posts` screen\n- User navigates back to the `/posts/123/edit` screen again\n\nWithout notifying your mutation management library about the route change, it's possible that your submission state could still be around and your user would still see the **\"Post updated successfully\"** message when they return to the previous screen. This is not ideal. Obviously, our intent wasn't to keep this mutation state around forever, right?!\n\n## Using mutation keys\n\nHopefully and hypothetically, the easiest way is for your mutation library to support a keying mechanism that will allow your mutations's state to be reset when the key changes:\n\n```tsx\nconst routeApi = getRouteApi('/room/$roomId/chat')\n\nfunction ChatRoom() {\n  const { roomId } = routeApi.useParams()\n\n  const sendMessageMutation = useCoolMutation({\n    fn: sendMessage,\n    // Clear the mutation state when the roomId changes\n    // including any submission state\n    key: ['sendMessage', roomId],\n  })\n\n  // Fire off a bunch of messages\n  const test = () => {\n    sendMessageMutation.mutate({ roomId, message: 'Hello!' })\n    sendMessageMutation.mutate({ roomId, message: 'How are you?' })\n    sendMessageMutation.mutate({ roomId, message: 'Goodbye!' })\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      {sendMessageMutation.submissions.map((submission) => {\n        return (\n          <div>\n            <div>{submission.status}</div>\n            <div>{submission.message}</div>\n          </div>\n        )\n      })}\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Using the `router.subscribe` method\n\nSee the [Router Events guide](./router-events.md) for a more complete walkthrough of the available events and when to use them.\n\nFor libraries that don't have a keying mechanism, we'll likely need to manually reset the mutation state when the user navigates away from the screen. To solve this, we can use TanStack Router's `invalidate` and `subscribe` method to clear mutation states when the user is no longer in need of them.\n\nThe `router.subscribe` method is a function that subscribes a callback to various router events. The event in particular that we'll use here is the `onResolved` event. It's important to understand that this event is fired when the location path is _changed (not just reloaded) and has finally resolved_.\n\nThis is a great place to reset your old mutation states. Here's an example:\n\n```tsx\nconst router = createRouter()\nconst coolMutationCache = createCoolMutationCache()\n\nconst unsubscribeFn = router.subscribe('onResolved', () => {\n  // Reset mutation states when the route changes\n  coolMutationCache.clear()\n})\n```\n"}
